    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>Business Analyst and Product Designer at <strong>Fortunesoft IT Innovations</strong>, operating at the intersection of product, design, and AI engineering — the profile top Indian AI labs hire for. I translate ambiguous AI product requirements into shippable systems, working across FinTech, Healthcare, and Life Sciences.</p>
    <p><a href="/resume.pdf" class="link-underline">View resume (PDF)</a></p>

    <h2 class="hs">What top AI labs hire for — and how my BA/PD work maps to it</h2>
    <p>Frontier AI companies (Sarvam, Krutrim, and global peers like Anthropic, OpenAI) don't just hire model builders — they hire <strong>product-minded practitioners who bridge the gap between model capabilities and shippable features</strong>. Their roles cluster around six themes that map directly to what a Business Analyst and Product Designer does every day — just with AI-native tooling.</p>
    <div class="g3">
      <div class="c"><h3>Requirements → Eval-Driven Specs</h3><p>Top labs practice "eval-driven development" — writing evaluations before code. As a BA, I do the same: turning ambiguous AI requirements into testable acceptance criteria, edge cases, and measurable success criteria before engineering starts.</p></div>
      <div class="c"><h3>Product Ownership → Shipping AI Features</h3><p>Top labs hire product managers who own end-to-end AI features. I do the same: scoping releases, managing change requests, deciding what each release needs — with AI-native tooling (evals, guardrails, prompt versioning).</p></div>
      <div class="c"><h3>Design Engineering → Prototyping AI Interfaces</h3><p>Frontier labs hire engineers for AI interfaces (chat, RAG, voice, multimodal). I wireframe and prototype AI interactions so conversations with ML engineers and designers stay concrete. I speak the language of the stack.</p></div>
      <div class="c"><h3>Delivery Governance → Visible AI Delivery</h3><p>Frontier labs run "eval-driven development" with visible status. I run Jira, workflows, quality tracking — visible status for client and team. AI projects need visibility into data quality, model drift, eval regressions, not just ticket status.</p></div>
      <div class="c"><h3>Systems Thinking → AI System Design</h3><p>Top labs hire systems engineers for inference, kernels, HPC. I map requirements to system boundaries: where does the model end and the product begin? Where do guardrails live? What's the fallback when the model hallucinates?</p></div>
      <div class="c"><h3>Eval-Driven Iteration → Continuous Improvement</h3><p>Frontier labs run "eval-driven development." I run the same loop: ship → measure (evals, user feedback, drift) → refine prompts/guardrails/RAG → re-eval. The loop is the product.</p></div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="hs">Where I align — current work at Fortunesoft</h2>
    <div class="g2">
      <div class="c"><h3>Requirements & Discovery</h3><p>Turning stakeholder conversations, specs and edge cases into scenarios the whole team agrees on before anything is built. AI products need this more than most — ambiguity compounds through the model.</p></div>
      <div class="c"><h3>Product Ownership</h3><p>Scoping phases, managing change requests, deciding what each release needs — from MVP to Phase 2. AI products need iteration speed with safety gates (evals, guardrails, human review).</p></div>
      <div class="c"><h3>Delivery Governance</h3><p>Jira, workflows, quality tracking — visible status for client and team. AI projects need visibility into data quality, model drift, eval regressions, not just ticket status.</p></div>
      <div class="c"><h3>Design Engineering</h3><p>Wireframing, prototyping, building small apps — so conversations with ML engineers, infra teams, and designers stay concrete. I speak the language of the stack (Python, PyTorch, FastAPI, RAG, evals).</p></div>
    </div>

